00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.340
Okay, with that out of the
way, onto today's event.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.270
We've been hearing a lot
about artificial intelligence

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.890
and the power and perils of the
rapid advances in the field.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.500
One of the frontier and
open questions in the field

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.770
is whether we can build
computer systems, platforms,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.140
maybe even a robot that can empathize

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.170
and connect like we humans do.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.260
This still remains a challenge.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.370
Meanwhile, we are also finally
paying a lot more attention

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.570
to animal intelligence and
communicating with them.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.170
The wonderful recent film,
"My Octopus Teacher",

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.170
demonstrates that there is an
entire unexplored living world

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.520
out there under the sea.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.540
Creatures from whom we have
much to learn and understand,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.690
both about ourselves and
our wonderful planet.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.350
I'm utterly delighted that Pippa Ehrlich,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:27.350
the co-writer, director and
editor of "My Octopus Teacher",

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.190
is here with us today.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.120
Welcome, Pippa.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.580
It's great to have you here at Yale.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.820
Thank you for coming.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.903
(audience applauds)

