00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.537
The Academy Award-winning film,

00:03:04.537 --> 00:03:06.720
"My Octopus Teacher" follows the story

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.710
of the years Craig Foster
spent with a wild octopus.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.200
Mr. Foster, suffering
from a loss of purpose,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.860
begins a daily diving regimen
in the freezing kelp forest

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.310
at the tip of Africa in
order to re-energize himself.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.010
What he discovers below
the water's surface

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.040
is a totally alien motivation in the form

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.160
of an unusually curious octopus.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.640
This beautiful record of
an animal's entire life,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.030
something seldom achieved in
the wild, let alone underwater,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.360
was shot over an entire
year and explores the habits

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.840
and personality of a
strange undulating creature

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.220
that most of us, well not all
of us, have only ever eaten.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.680
Filmed with the cutting edge six K camera,

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.390
some of the world's top filmmakers,

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.180
cinematographers and
scientists came together

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.360
to create this incredibly remarkable film,

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.430
which marks the first underwater movie,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:02.400
combining high-end, blue chip
natural history sequences

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.130
with the story of a
human/animal relationship

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.870
documenting the individual creature

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.230
throughout its lifetime in the wild.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.040
The film has of course garnered
a great amount of attention

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.980
from conservationists,
scientists all over the world,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.210
as well as celebrities,

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:21.030
and it has received more
than 20 international awards,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.880
including an Oscar, a
BAFTA, the prestigious

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.480
Wildscreen Golden Panda, Jackson's
Wildes Grand Teton Award,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.380
the (indistinct), and the
Producers Guild of America Award

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.630
for the best documentary motion picture.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.330
For the last six years,
Pippa has been part

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.670
of the Sea Change Project, exploring

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.980
the underwater forest of Cape Town.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.200
Prior to joining Sea Change, Pippa worked

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.610
as an environmental journalist,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.830
specializing in the
field of marine science

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.100
and conservation and the
intersection between people

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.570
in the natural world.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.130
She has worked with some of the world's

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.040
top marine researchers and
underwater photographers

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.560
and has been an avid free diver herself

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.330
for more than a decade.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.870
So I am delighted.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.695
Before that, I would like to
give a super special thank you

00:05:07.695 --> 00:05:11.520
to two people who worked really extra hard

