I have a couple of my
own, but I'll hold back.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:04.147
So I see an arm go up promptly,
and so we have a roving mic.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.150
So yes, thank you.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.770
- My voice is loud. So I
think you probably hear,

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.713
but I will also project through this.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.070
First, congratulations on the film.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:25.070
Obviously it's been very, very accolade.

00:09:25.083 --> 00:09:26.880
It's got many accolades
and very well awarded

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.020
and very well deserved.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.070
And I'm sure you've heard that

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.063
a million times.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:37.302
But I wanted to know ,when
he says you were a visitor

00:09:37.302 --> 00:09:41.940
and we're not just a visitor,
we're a part of this.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:46.050
Did you ever feel like there's
Craig or did he even feel

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.473
or relate to you that, you know,

00:09:47.473 --> 00:09:49.530
I feel like I'm encroaching on their space

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:54.530
and I am changing how the camera may.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.500
I'm an actor, so the
camera con always changes

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.950
the way you look, it's just natural.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:06.120
So it would change the behaviors
of the different wildlife

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.120
that you were coming
into interaction with.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.470
Is that something that
you were cognizant of

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:15.060
or is that something that you
got more comfortable with,

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:16.680
if that's at all possible?

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.633
Or is it just, was it a daily
struggle maybe, just to.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:27.630
- I mean, I think these are
things where you're evolving

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:32.460
all the time as an, as an
individual and as a storyteller.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.100
And it was something that
I was very conscious of

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.300
the whole time because I'd come

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.780
from a very scientific, journalistic place

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.850
where engaging with animals like this

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.733
was actually completely alien to me.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:51.930
But when I started diving
with Craig and seeing

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.330
how he engaged with animals
and seeing how animals

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:59.330
actually approached him,
many of these creatures,

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.650
they don't have to be there.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:04.383
He's not gonna go and
kind of chase them down.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:12.600
And I guess my attitude
started to change slightly.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.810
And I still, I think
it's very, very complex.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.453
I think you do change their behavior.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.880
I think in some cases it's negative.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.580
In some cases it's not, because they are

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:27.240
as curious as you are,
and with most creatures

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.800
that live underwater, they
can swim and move a lot better

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.423
in the ocean than we can.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.050
But what I'm increasingly conscious of

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.150
is what kind of message
are you putting out?

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:47.150
Because Craig is incredibly
experienced, respectful,

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.213
and kind of loving towards these animals.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.990
But I don't know if you
can always expect everyone

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:56.790
to go into natural spaces like that

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:59.010
and more and more in our work,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.563
because I still work
with Sea Change Project.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.110
I'm not sure today if we
would make a film like this

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.093
because we've seen so much more.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:14.970
But that said, I think that
separating people from nature

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.643
is where all the problems
have started anyway.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.513
- Why would you film like
this again, just for the.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:29.540
- Well, because I think it is complicated.

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.733
How close should we be
getting to the natural world?

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.280
- So you do feel like the
negatives outweigh the positives?

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:39.401
- No.

00:12:39.401 --> 00:12:40.537
= After doing it? No.

00:12:40.537 --> 00:12:41.462
- No, not at all.

00:12:41.462 --> 00:12:42.735
Yeah, 'cause I didn't feel.

00:12:42.735 --> 00:12:43.568
- [Priya] It's real that
the negatives are real

00:12:43.568 --> 00:12:46.860
because you are perturbing,
you know, their life cycle,

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:50.100
you're intervening in
a way that, you know,

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.862
it's not entirely clear if you're welcome.

00:12:51.862 --> 00:12:52.695
- Right.

00:12:52.695 --> 00:12:53.886
- Right.
- Exactly.

00:12:53.886 --> 00:12:54.967
- So it's an intervention.

00:12:54.967 --> 00:12:56.910
