screen, so it was beautiful.

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:41.000
And I had a question for you.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:44.160
So you were talking about
the narrative and editing,

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.870
and I was hoping you
could speak a little bit

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.400
to the role of gender in the film,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.790
because obviously this
is a female octopus.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.160
That's revealed at the
end when she gives birth.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:55.200
But I noticed the entire time,

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.570
Craig sort of refers to her as she,

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.430
and I think it's sort of interesting

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:01.440
because you have this imposition of a very

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.750
sort of human idea and almost,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.230
I sensed a bit of almost
a kind of like at times,

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.330
paternal at times, romantic way

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:11.070
that he's framing this relationship,

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:13.080
which I think really does
add something to the film,

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:16.110
but perhaps at the cost
of imposing, as I said,

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:18.450
a sort of human idea of gender

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:21.960
or of relations onto these animals.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.240
So I'm just curious if you
could talk a little bit more

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.670
about the positives and negatives

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:29.295
or anything you wanna
say about the choice.

00:18:29.295 --> 00:18:31.650
- He wasn't gonna call the octopus it.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.620
I mean, in no universe
you can see the kind

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:38.700
of respect and reverence
he has for that animal.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:41.913
He was never gonna say it
did this and it did that.

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:46.110
And she was dead, you
know, two or three years

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:47.670
before we did the interview.

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:52.650
So he'd always known
that she was a female.

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:56.070
So I mean, were we imposing anything?

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:59.460
I think we were just telling a story

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:01.593
where the animal happened to be female.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:05.913
What does it mean?

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:08.363
I'm not sure.

00:19:08.363 --> 00:19:12.870
I think if the octopus had been male

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.663
and there hadn't been this
kind of story of motherhood,

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:18.030
we would've ended up with a very,

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:20.400
very kind of masculine film.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.340
So I think that we were
actually really lucky

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:28.340
that the energy of the octopus
was there to balance out

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:32.550
this other very strong
father/son narrative

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:33.810
which went through the film.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:37.200
And you know, again, that
that wasn't a creative choice.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.500
Craig just happened to
have a boy, not a girl.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.060
- Right. I guess just to be clear,

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:45.390
I didn't mean in the sense of
like a dehumanizing effect,

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.100
but more just I think
we're trying to think

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.860
in binaristic different ways about gender

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.080
and what that means and the relationship

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:55.380
between sex and gender. So
more in that sense, but.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:56.280
- yeah, thank you.

00:19:57.225 --> 00:19:58.058
Very interesting.

00:19:58.058 --> 00:20:02.670
- This question and then throw
back where you got it from.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:03.723
- Yeah. Oh God.

00:20:09.363 --> 00:20:10.363
- Thank you.

00:20:11.199 --> 00:20:13.470
Hi. Thank you for hosting this event,

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:14.430
and thank you for coming.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.200
And this is my first
time watching the film,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:20.220
and I was so impressed
