00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.110
the narrative sequences were.

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:26.310
And like those chase
sequences were almost,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.560
they reminded me of
"The French Connection"

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.480
and how well crafted they were

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:32.220
and all these like shot reverse shots

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.800
between the octopus and Craig.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.310
I am wondering more like
from a technical perspective,

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.520
how much was shot after
the fact and how much

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:44.580
was Greg's original footage? Yeah.

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:49.200
- So pretty much anytime you
see Craig without the octopus,

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:50.463
that was filmed later,

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:56.610
what was quite amazing in
certainly the chase sequences

00:20:56.610 --> 00:20:59.100
and many of the hunting sequences,

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:03.600
Craig was on one camera
and Roger Herrock's friend,

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.750
who shot most of the
underwater, was on a RAID.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.060
So that's how you have
those reverse angles.

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:10.308
- Right.

00:21:10.308 --> 00:21:11.880
- You've got Craig with
his little tiny handycam

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.323
like racing around and
Roger on his big raid setup.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:18.750
- That's super impressive.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.480
- That's fantastic given
that there was no shadow.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.580
I was looking really hard to see, I mean,

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:24.660
who's the filmer, right?

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:25.560
Where's the shadow?

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.090
- I saw a shadow once.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:27.923
- You did?

00:21:27.923 --> 00:21:29.191
- Which I'd never noticed before.

00:21:29.191 --> 00:21:30.720
I only saw tonight
because of the big screen.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:32.220
(laughter)

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:33.663
It was a flash.

00:21:35.106 --> 00:21:36.814
- I didn't see it.

00:21:36.814 --> 00:21:38.153
- Let's see. I definitely didn't catch it.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.750
- So I did a similar thing
this summer with deer,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:58.860
a family of deer that were
out where I used to run.

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:03.860
Not here, in Connecticut,
but I would go back every day

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:09.050
and I found that in watching
them how they react to me,

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:14.990
I started seeing more
instinctual behavior by humans,

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:20.490
particularly how we look
at or regard strangers

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:24.210
who were coming at us and
the whole predator/prey thing

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:25.230
and that sort of thing.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:30.230
And so I'm wondering if his
experience with those animals

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:35.690
had resulted in him gaining
insight on human beings

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.473
and how instinctual we are.

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:43.530
- Very much so.

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:44.859
- Yeah.

00:22:44.859 --> 00:22:49.859
- I think during this
time, and I see Craig

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.130
do it with other animals now,

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:54.750
anyone who understands animals well

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:55.860
is doing this all the time.

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.890
And you're constantly
moving out of human mind

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:03.570
into animal mind as best you
can because we are humans.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:05.190
And that's the framework through which

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:06.390
we understand the world.

00:23:07.372 --> 00:23:11.220
And there's a lot of criticism
about anthropomorphism,

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:15.240
but the truth is that
every single living thing

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.480
on this planet wants to live.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.790
And there's the dichotomy
between life or death.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:24.990
And that means that we've
gained all of these instincts.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:27.930
And in the human world, those
instincts have translated

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.720
into emotions, but ultimately

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:33.990
it's the same kind of
physiological driving forces

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.610
that are making us feel these things.

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:40.610
So I get quite frustrated
when people say things like,

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:42.930
you know, an octopus can't feel emotions

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:47.190
because why we think our emotions,

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:50.250
which are really just survival instincts,

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:53.410
are above what an animal experiences

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:57.517
is really just extremely arrogant.

00:23:57.517 --> 00:24:00.510
- And I thought it was just
follow up in terms of him

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:04.290
not wanting to intervene
between, there's a shark.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.443
It's like he's almost like, well,

00:24:07.443 --> 00:24:10.740
I'm in the position of God here.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:12.750
- No, I thought it was very self-conscious

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:15.079
because he said, "I
shouldn't intervene in nature

00:24:15.079 --> 00:24:15.912
the order of."

00:24:15.912 --> 00:24:17.190
- But if he's part of, this is my point,

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.380
if he's part of nature, if he's in there,

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:22.740
if he's not a guest, if he's in it,

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:26.103
then him intervening in that,
he's part of the system.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:30.754
- He's not part of the natural
order of the system, right.

00:24:30.754 --> 00:24:32.400
So this is not his habitat.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:36.090
- I think he is though, but
he's in there, he's swimming.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:37.143
That's natural.

00:24:39.141 --> 00:24:42.323
- I mean, this was the biggest thing I was

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:50.110
kind of confused about
while making the film

00:24:51.005 --> 00:24:52.710
and that was the choice he made,

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:55.080
it's not necessarily the
choice I would've made.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:56.316
- Correct.

00:24:56.316 --> 00:24:57.149
- And I found it confusing.

00:24:57.149 --> 00:24:59.940
Okay, well you're not gonna
save it from the shark,

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.733
but you think it's okay
to feed it mussels.

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:05.463
Feed her mussels.

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:12.030
And for Craig it makes sense.

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:14.310
And I think that's what we do as humans.

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.260
You know, some of us say, well,

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.810
I'm not gonna eat octopus
because I saw this amazing movie,

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:21.789
but I'm gonna keep eating
lamb and chicken and cows.

00:25:21.789 --> 00:25:22.739
- Right.

00:25:22.739 --> 00:25:23.572
- Exactly.

00:25:23.572 --> 00:25:25.140
- And we are all making
these personal decisions

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.810
that feel right to us,
and they are hypocritical

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:29.850
and they don't make perfect sense.

00:25:29.850 --> 00:25:32.280
- Yeah, and they're often
contradictory, right.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:36.527
I mean, I'm a vegetarian and have not

00:25:36.527 --> 00:25:41.527
eaten any animal knowingly, but
I have squashed ants, right.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:44.133
I mean, I do, I mean.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:46.320
I'm more gentle with spiders.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.860
I ask them to leave and I
gently, you know, but ants,

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.300
I'm always squashing.

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:52.353
- And mosquitoes?

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:56.670
- I've never been lucky in squatting

00:25:56.670 --> 00:26:00.660
any of them or swatting
any of them, I think.

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:03.120
Okay. We will close with one final, okay.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:05.610
Maybe two final questions
because I don't wanna keep you

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.463
much longer from the yummy
food that we have upstairs.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:11.700
- Steven.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:12.533
- Okay.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:16.176
- Thank you.

00:26:16.176 --> 00:26:18.420
It's a really extraordinary movie.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.660
My second time viewing it,
and seeing it on a big screen

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.260
really amplifies so much
of the incredible messaging

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:27.300
that you've created I think, in this film.

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:31.950
One of the things that sticks
with me is consciousness.

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:35.610
Is how this film, and I think things

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:37.680
that really change our
perception of the world

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:39.780
is the way we see it.

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:43.050
And I think one of the things
that I was struck with,

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:45.707
was early in the film,
the way you set it up

00:26:45.707 --> 00:26:50.100
with his trip to Africa and
acknowledging that these guys

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.350
were seeing things differently
than he could ever see them.

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:55.260
And there was this sense
of like, he wanted that,

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:57.870
he wanted to have that
depth of connection.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:00.600
Do you feel that in making this film,

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:05.254
and I know you had your
background in the sciences,

00:27:05.254 --> 00:27:08.040
did you find your perception,

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.560
your consciousness has
changed towards nature

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:13.380
just in the process of making the film?

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:15.030
- Absolutely.

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.210
I think just making this film,

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:21.210
I was spending two, three
hours a day underwater

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:26.160
with those animals and even
without an indigenous lens,

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:29.040
which is something that I'm
very, very interested in now

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.573
and exploring very deeply,

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:34.980
just spending that much
time surrounded by nature

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.050
in that way and kind of putting yourself

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:40.680
at the mercy of it, at
the mercy of big waves,

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:45.680
big sharks, currents, that
completely changes you.

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:52.650
And yeah, it just, in some ways
things become much simpler,

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:55.263
but then in other ways
more complex, I suppose.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:28:00.150
- Yeah, I can imagine that
it makes you feel vulnerable

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.570
and yet stronger somehow,
because you kind of survive

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:06.420
every time you go and you come
back out successfully, right.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:07.416
Maybe.

00:28:07.416 --> 00:28:10.320
I can only imagine.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:13.320
There's one final
question before we close.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:14.647
We just pass the mic.

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:36.713
- Thank you.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:38.393
- Thank you.

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:41.310
Thank you so much for being here.

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:43.650
Thank you so much for the film.

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:45.900
This is probably a bad last question,

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.100
but I was wondering like,

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:49.530
could you talk about like
the genesis of the project?

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:51.420
Like how you found the story

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:53.553
or how the story found you, maybe.

00:28:55.251 --> 00:28:56.400
- It's quite a long story,

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:58.563
but I'm gonna try and speed it up.

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:03.210
I was working as a conservation journalist

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:06.213
for a marine conservation NGO.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:10.800
And I was speaking to all
these incredible scientists

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.980
all over the world, and I
was hearing their stories

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:18.060
of how they were working
with these animals

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:20.100
and immersing in environments

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.520
and having these magical experiences.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:24.990
And two things happened.

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:28.470
Number one, I became a
little bit frustrated

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:30.903
that they would tell me
these things on a call,

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:33.780
but if ever it was an interview

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:36.780
or I asked them to write
a blog or anything else,

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:40.173
they took all the emotion out
of everything that they wrote.

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:43.350
And I found as someone
who was really trying

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:45.450
to make a difference in the world

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:49.320
and bring the natural
world to life, I was like,

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.390
if the people who are closest to nature

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:53.940
are unwilling to talk
about how they really feel

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:56.703
about nature, what chance do we stand?

00:29:57.810 --> 00:29:59.610
So that was the first thing.

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:01.920
And the second thing was I started feeling

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:06.920
like I was vicariously
experiencing all these things

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:10.930
and I desperately wanted
to have my own connection

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.143
to the natural world.

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:18.570
And Craig was doing
talks all over Cape Town,

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:19.470
not about the octopus,

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.930
but just about the kelp forest in general.

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:24.810
And I ended up going diving with him

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.103
through a mutual friend.

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:32.220
And yeah, about six months later we'd done

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:34.620
a couple of small projects together

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:37.017
and he asked if I would like
to help him make the film.

00:30:37.017 --> 00:30:38.520
And at that point I really thought

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:40.233
I would just be carrying a tripod.

00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:43.088
I did not expect it to evolve

00:30:43.088 --> 00:30:45.693
the way that it did on any level.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:51.910
- Fantastic. Thank you
so much for coming and

00:30:53.027 --> 00:30:56.110
(audience applauds)

